Z||000009||jhar1987||08-07-2000||09:50 PM||||ok i will do the 3800 swap, but do i need the trans from the bonniville or the fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000010||3400Fiero||08-07-2000||11:25 PM||||I did a 3400 DOHC swap into my 1988 formula without a kit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  The most important thing is having the factory wiring diagrams and patience! I don't know about the tranny since the 3400 can bolt right up to any fiero tranny using the stock (1988) V6 flywheel.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||08-08-2000||08:49 PM||||Wait!, those aren't wires, they're little smoke tubes! If you do everything right, the smoke will stay inside, but if you connect a wrong wire you're gonna have a smoke leak! <P>I've done a 3.8 Fiero, a 5.7 TPI Fiero, a 5.7 TBI S-10, and I'm working on a 4.9 Fiero. GM harnesses are not too tough to figure out. A schematic is a must have, preferably a factory manual. Go for it!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||12
Z||000012||terryk||08-08-2000||10:35 PM||||Where possible, always use the trans that came with the engine. If the engine came with a 4T60E, use the 4T60E. If it camewith a 4T65E, use the 4T65E. If the engine was available with a manual (the 3800 wasn't) then go ahead and use a manual and order the manual trans mem-cal.<P>As a note, the 92-93 3800's could support a 125C or 440T4/4T60 with the correct calibration changes. The 94 and up doesn't.<P>You'd be surprised what the ECM will do when the wrong tranny is used.<P>Flame suit on....<P>As for the wiring, the others have pretty much covered it. The factory manuals/diagrams are a must. Watch out for the engine having oil pressure and water temp switches instead of senders (the Fiero want's senders). <P>Get a Fiero harness and then match the equivalent wires on the 3800 harness. Use the Fiero body connectors and the 3800 engine connectors. Try to use the same fuses to supply the same circuit. Be prepared to use larger fuses in some cases. Solder all connections. Shrink tubing is preferred to electrical tape. <P>Terry<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-08-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000013||GT Bastard||08-08-2000||11:40 PM||||Terryk, what kind of problems can you expect to run into if you tried to use a 5sp Getrag with a 3800 conversion?||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||08-09-2000||01:21 AM||||You will get the trans error codes (26, 36, 38, 39, and 56 or their ODB I/II variants). Most can be solved with 10 and 20 ohm resistors. <P>Next, you may get hesitation/stalling during shifts. The 3800SC ECM doesn't allow full power WOT shifts on the automatic so you could see some power reduction or a delay in power just after the shift. You may get stalling if you let the throttle snap closed since there won't be any stall saver/shift anticipation.<P>I need to stop writing these boring essays so if you want more information, email me offline. I'd be more than happy to give you some suggestions.<P>The 92-95 SC is one of my sweet spots!<P>Terry<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Dan86gt3.8||08-09-2000||03:37 PM||||ya terryk know's his $hit!!!!!!!!  and what is the 4.9?  Is that a cadilac motor?||||reg||1
Z||000016||rockcrawl||08-09-2000||04:55 PM||||The 4.9 is a 250 Hp 275 lb/ft torque, aluminum Cadillac V8 with sequential port fuel injection. Same bolt pattern as the 2.8, 3.8, etc., same weight as the 2.8.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||14
