Z||000020||Dan86gt3.8||08-10-2000||11:50 AM||||OK I don't mena to flame anybody but arent 250hp/275tq the same numbers the 3.8 series 2 s/c puts out.  especially with the smaller pulley to up the boost?  I trhought I heard a series 2 (vin "K") motor with the pulley was supposed to make around 300 hp.  Or it just might with headers and bolt on stuff.  <P>Ohhh and on a side note I think the hp is even more underated.  If you think about it the engine only came in big gm cars.  Well when you out it in the fiero you cut out the power steerting pump, you free up the exhaust big time, and sometimes ditch the ac.  And all thoose accesories add drag as you may well know, and I think the exhaust system on mine is alot freer (more free) than it was in the olds ninety-eight that the engine came in originally.  None of this is scientificaly proven I was just thinking outloud some....||||reg||1
Z||000021||terryk||08-10-2000||11:55 AM||||Series II's are 240HP/280ft lbs. Personally, I think that is generous. I have a Series I 225HP/265 ft-lbs in a Ciera GT and it will take my wife's 2000 GTP. The GTP is just tons more civilized. SC engines are VIN 1, K's are NA.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Phil||08-10-2000||12:02 PM||||My SC series II was dyno'ed and gave 222hp and 275 ft-lb at the wheels. I do have the small pulley on the blower. Thrasher Racing gets 309 hp with intercooler, special cam, headers and headwork( not in a Fiero though)||||reg||1
Z||000023||terryk||08-10-2000||12:21 PM||||Pumped, I agree that the Series II can clear 300HP. There is a 455HP Series II in the 2000 SSEi heading for Bonneville to break the land speed record. Head work, cam, 2.56 gears, exhaust, etc. 209.81 MPH is the record.<P>There are ported/polished/balanced Series I engines getting 290+HP. The trick is on being able to add more spark and water injection. Just 2 little degrees of additional spark really helps.<P>What's nice is that a Series II will run on a 94/95 ECM which allows calibration changes via the mem-cal (unlike the 96 and up flash PCM's that are limited to a few people recalibrating them). But you can only use a 4T60E. <P>Use the smaller pulley, water injection and 280+ should be possible on a Series II and still have pistons when you are done.  <P>I've seen the Thrasher intercooler and it's just too much to deal with. I've considered intercooling my Series I and opted for water injection.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||3400Fiero||08-10-2000||12:35 PM||||If you are interested in gaining the most from your engine by modifying the spark timing and fuel curves etc, why not get an aftermarket engine management computer that will allow you to modify to your hearts content?  That is what I am planning for my 3400 once I get the money!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000025||terryk||08-10-2000||01:43 PM||||For me, it's because the aftermarket controllers aren't anywhere near as comprehensive as the OEM.<P>Terry||||reg||1
